  {
   "id": "one_step_evolution",
   "description": "Integrates the Routing equations as a continuous PDE over one dt = 0.25 yr update step and asserts W_out, W_till_out, the time-averaged advective flux components flux_u/flux_v (the C++ Qstag_average/dt mapped staggered-to-regular), and P_over at the dome center (16,16), the inner ring (r = 58 km), the margin (r ~ 210-580 km), and the far field (r ~ 656 km), against stage1/dumps/hydrology/columns.csv. The C++ integrates the same semi-discrete system with 19 explicit Euler substeps capped by the CFL limit (max_timestep_W_cfl, ~4.8-day steps), so the one-step agreement carries the Euler-vs-ODE scheme gap, measured here at up to 4.4% relative for W_out and the flux (the dome, where the drainage is fastest, and the far field, where the flux grows ~1000x over the step); W_till_out agrees to the solver tolerance (rel 1e-6): the till rate surface + basal - C is time-independent, so the Euler substeps telescope to the continuous solution and the dry till absorbing the input is pinned near-exactly. The C++ projects W and W_till to zero at ice-free cells after each substep (enforce_bounds); the continuous esm form keeps the tiny blob tail (<= 3e-30 m) and the water that drains onto the ice-free margin ring (~1e-6 m over the step) in place, so only grounded cells are asserted. Tolerances: rel 0.05 for W and the nonzero flux components (measured max 4.4%, ~1.1x margin), abs 1e-23 for the exactly-zero flux components on the symmetry axes (esm noise ~1e-27..1e-30), rel 1e-6 for W_till, rel 1e-12 for P_over.",
